course: dictionary
lesson: 7744
title: Puns and wordplay
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pun is a joke that uses a word with two meanings, or two words that sound the same, to make people laugh. Puns are a kind of wordplay, and you can find them in jokes, riddles, headlines and poems.
explain: Many puns use homophones, words that sound the same but mean different things, like flour and flower. Others use one word that has two meanings in the same sentence. When you hear a joke that sounds odd, ask yourself: is there a second meaning hiding in one of the words?
explain: The Duchess in Alice's story says, «Talking of axes, chop off her head!» Axes can mean chopping tools, and the word sounds like axis, so the line is a pun. Later she says, «I never could abide figures!» Abide means stand, and figures means numbers, so the joke is about maths.
explain: «A pun that makes everyone groan has still worked; the groan is part of the fun.» People say «pun intended» when they know they have made a pun on purpose. A very bad pun is called a groaner.
fact: A pun is a joke using a word or phrase with two meanings, or two words that sound the same.
fact: To pun means to make such a joke.
fact: A homophone is a word that sounds the same as another word but has a different meaning, like flour and flower.
fact: A groaner is a bad pun that makes people groan.
fact: A dad joke is a simple, corny pun, and part of the fun is that it is so bad.
exercise: The Duchess says «Talking of axes, chop off her head!» What is the pun? | *Axes means chopping tools, and it sounds like axis | Axes are the lines on a graph | A duchess is a kind of cake | Head is a sort of hat
exercise: In the Mock Turtle's school, «Reeling and Writhing» are the subjects. What do these puns stand for? | Singing and dancing | Sums and numbers | Fishing and swimming | *Reading and writing, which sound alike
exercise: The Duchess says «I never could abide figures!» What does abide mean here? | Pay for | Sing | Hide | *Stand
test: Which sentence is a pun? | «The cat sat on the mat and slept.» | «Please pass the salt.» | *«Why did the bread go to the doctor? It felt crumby.» | «We walked to school in the rain.»
test: What is a groaner? | A type of riddle with no answer | A very loud laugh | A sad poem about a dog | *A bad pun that makes people groan
exam: Why do people groan at a pun? | *Because it is so bad or clever in a silly way that it makes them groan | Because they are tired and want to go home | Because puns are always written in capital letters | Because the joke is about something sad
